{
    "template_id": "THRESHOLD_EPISODIC_BOUNDARY_001",
    "display_id": "TP2",
    "name": "Architectural Threshold as Episodic Boundary — The Doorway Effect and Memory Structure",
    "structural_pattern": "Crossing an architectural threshold triggers hippocampal context updating ('The Doorway Effect'). This segments continuous experience into discrete episodic memories. The strength of the boundary depends on the magnitude of multi-modal change (space, light, acoustics) across the threshold. CALIBRATION (TP-II): Boundary strength d ≈ Σ(d_channels) × 1.17 (super-additivity).",
    "higher_order_principle": "Spatial boundaries function as memory boundaries. Architecture structures the 'narrative' of memory by segmenting experience into chapters via thresholds. ATTENTIONAL BOOST: Thresholds trigger transient attention spikes, making the transition itself more memorable while the previous context is forgotten (The Paradox Ratio).",
    "transferable_to": [
        "Exhibition Design (segmenting thematic sections)",
        "Retail (transitional zones)",
        "Digital UX (page transitions as context shifts)"
    ],
    "framework_ids": [
        "event_segmentation_theory",
        "situation_models",
        "episodic_memory",
        "context_dependent_memory"
    ],
    "causal_links": [
